    {
      "id": "9262a632a15dda408965008f6a5425d425b2f15b",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 519739482,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> I don't know the situation exactly for Windows\n\n...\n\n> But I don't see the problem with Linux\n\n1. That's not a complete enumeration of the platforms on which we run.\n1. \"Linux\", from a GUI standpoint, isn't *a* platform.\n\nThe underlying issue is \"if you launch something from the GUI, with no command line involved, where does the standard error go?\"\n\nBased on some tests I did (making Wireshark's main do an `fstat()` on FD 2 and report on the file type and the appropriate information about that file type - node number of regular files, device major/minor for character special files, local and peer name for sockets, under the assumption that the socket is AF_UNIX, and then write a test message to stderr):\n\n- on macOS, the answer appears to be \"to `/dev/null`\" (character special with `/dev/null`'s major and minor);\n- on GNOME on Fedora 31, the answer appears to be \"a socket connection to the systemd journal logger\";\n- on KDE on Fedora 31, the answer appears to be \"~/.xsession-errors\".\n\nSo:\n\n- on macOS, stuff written to the standard error disappears without a trace;\n- on GNOME on Fedora 31, stuff written to the standard error might, in theory, show up somewhere that `journalctl` can find, but either I wasn't using it correctly or it's hidden somewhere\n- on KDE on Fedora 31, stuff written to the standard error shows up in your ~/.xsession-errors.\n\nI can't speak for other distributions, other desktop configurations on those distributions, or other desktop UN\\*Xes.\n\n> and even if it is a problem, I don't see how this checkAPI thing is a proper solution\n\nI didn't say it was.\n\n*Banning* logging debug/\"something that wasn't supposed to happen happened, and there's nothing the user or their site administrator can do about it, so they should report it to Somebody Else, such as the Wireshark developers or the Npcap developers or the OS supplier\" messages of *any* sort isn't useful.\n\n*Making sure those messages show up somewhere allowing the user to see them*, however, is.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-03-01T21:23:16.492Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-03-01T21:23:16.492Z",
