    {
      "id": "6e7a6e48f28b99aa71c82f94ca430d05a3062c7f",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 519763940,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> I was implicitly assuming that anyone interested in debug/diagnostic logging is savvy enough to run Wireshark from a command line.\n\nIf it's truly a *debugging* message, yes, but should those be present in shipping binary code and present by default in code compiled from shipping source, if whoever added the debugging messages fixed the problem?\n\n*Diagnostic logging*, however, should be made reasonably easy for users to get at, so that, if somebody's filed a bug, they can get at it and add it to the bug if we ask.\n\nFor example, that's what `REPORT_DISSECTOR_BUG()` is for - it shows up in the packet dissection.\n\nAnd for problems that the user might be able to address (for example, a configuration file that can't be opened, or that's malformed) or might be able to get somebody at their site to address, messages for that should show up through regular error mechanisms.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
